<!-- ——— Room II: the stone room. The livret. ——— -->
<section class="room room-stone sect" id="catalogue" aria-labelledby="cat-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <figure class="plate">
      <div class="mount"><img src="/salon/martini-wall.webp" width="2400" height="978" alt="An engraving of the Salon of 1787 at the Louvre: paintings hung frame to frame from the floor to the ceiling." loading="lazy"></div>
      <figcaption><i>Exposition au Salon du Louvre en 1787</i>, Pietro Antonio Martini. Every painting in the room had a number in the Salon's printed catalogue, so visitors could find what they were looking at.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  <div class="livret">

    <div>
      <p class="kicker"><span>II</span> The catalogue</p>
      <h2 id="cat-title">Everything you read, numbered and easy to find.</h2>
      <p class="intro">A crowded wall only works if you can find your way around it. Leaflogue keeps your passages in order: by book, by tag, by idea, by date, so the one you want is a search away.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <ol class="entries">
        <li><span class="n">1</span><div><h3>Import from your reader</h3><p>Plug it in and choose Import. Your reader is only read, never written to.</p><div class="readers"><span>Kobo</span><span>Kindle</span><span>PocketBook</span><span>Tolino</span><span>Boox</span><span>KOReader</span></div></div></li>
        <li><span class="n">2</span><div><h3>Add paper books by hand</h3><p>Type in a passage from a printed book. It sits alongside everything you imported.</p></div></li>
        <li><span class="n">3</span><div><h3>Notes beside the words</h3><p>Write in Markdown under any passage. Your thinking stays next to the line that started it.</p></div></li>
        <li><span class="n">4</span><div><h3>Tags and collections</h3><p>Label passages with tags, and group books into collections.</p></div></li>
        <li><span class="n">5</span><div><h3>Threads across books</h3><p>Follow one idea through several books, and read its passages together.</p></div></li>
        <li><span class="n">6</span><div><h3>Search everything</h3><p>Find a passage from a half-remembered phrase, in the passages and in your notes.</p></div></li>
        <li><span class="n">7</span><div><h3>One passage a day</h3><p>Something you once kept comes back each day, so good lines aren't forgotten.</p></div></li>
        <li><span class="n">8</span><div><h3>Your reading, in numbers</h3><p>See the authors you keep underlining and your busy and quiet months. No streaks to keep up.</p></div></li>
        <li><span class="n">9</span><div><h3>Ask about a passage</h3><p>Off until you turn it on. The first time, you see exactly what will be sent, then you can talk the passage through.</p></div></li>
        <li><span class="n">10</span><div><h3>Open it in a browser</h3><p>Run the web app on your own server to reach the same library from anywhere.</p></div></li>
        <li><span class="n">11</span><div><h3>Look up a word offline</h3><p>English definitions and synonyms, bundled with Leaflogue desktop. No internet, account, or AI key.</p></div></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ——— Room III: the green room. On the line. ——— -->
<section class="room room-green sect" aria-labelledby="line-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="kicker"><span>III</span> On the line</p>
    <h2 id="line-title">The passage, and what you made of it.</h2>
    <p class="intro">At the Salon, the best paintings were hung “on the line”, at eye level, where nobody could miss them. Leaflogue does the same for what you read: the words first, with your notes, tags and threads around them.</p>
    <div class="line-hang">
      <figure class="piece main">
        <div class="mount shot">
          <img class="shot-light" src="/salon/desktop-highlights-light.webp" width="1440" height="1000" alt="The Highlights page: totals across the top, filters by book on the left, and passages with notes in a long list." loading="lazy">
          <img class="shot-dark" src="/salon/desktop-highlights-dark.webp" width="1440" height="1000" alt="The Highlights page in the dark theme." loading="lazy">
          <img class="phone-shot" src="/salon/phone-passage-light.webp" width="780" height="1688" alt="Leaflogue on a phone: a passage with its note." loading="lazy">
        </div>
        <figcaption class="cartel"><span class="no">No. 12</span><span><strong>Highlights.</strong> Every passage and note in your library, with filters by book, notes and tags.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="line-side">
      <figure class="piece side1">
        <div class="mount shot"><img src="/salon/thread-panel-light.webp" width="1436" height="768" alt="A thread called How to live, joining a passage from Walden and one from Meditations." loading="lazy"></div>
        <figcaption class="cartel"><span class="no">No. 13</span><span><strong>Threads.</strong> One idea, followed through two very different books.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="piece side2">
        <div class="mount shot"><img src="/salon/search-life-light.webp" width="1226" height="640" alt="Searching for the word life, with matching passages from Pride and Prejudice and Frankenstein." loading="lazy"></div>
        <figcaption class="cartel"><span class="no">No. 14</span><span><strong>Search.</strong> Type a word you remember and the passage comes back.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="piece side3">
        <div class="mount shot"><img src="/salon/import-kobo-light.webp" width="1656" height="354" alt="The Import page noticing that a Kobo is plugged in, with an Import from Kobo button." loading="lazy"></div>
        <figcaption class="cartel"><span class="no">No. 15</span><span><strong>Import.</strong> Leaflogue notices your reader as soon as it's plugged in.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>
      </div>
      <figure class="piece under2">
        <div class="mount shot">
          <img class="shot-light" src="/salon/desktop-statistics-light.webp" width="1440" height="1000" alt="The Statistics page: totals, a calendar of reading days, and charts by month and weekday." loading="lazy" style="aspect-ratio: 1440/445; object-fit: cover; object-position: top;">
          <img class="shot-dark" src="/salon/desktop-statistics-dark.webp" width="1440" height="1000" alt="The Statistics page in the dark theme." loading="lazy" style="aspect-ratio: 1440/445; object-fit: cover; object-position: top;">
        </div>
        <figcaption class="cartel"><span class="no">No. 16</span><span><strong>Statistics.</strong> Drawn only from what's already in your library.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="dictionary-feature" id="offline-dictionary">
      <div class="dictionary-copy">
        <p class="kicker">A word, without a detour</p>
        <h3>Stay with the passage.<br><em>Find the meaning.</em></h3>
        <p>Select a word and choose Define. English definitions, parts of speech, and synonyms are already on your computer, ready even when the internet is not.</p>
        <p class="dictionary-detail">Included in Leaflogue desktop · No account, AI key, or separate download</p>
        <a class="dictionary-guide" href="/library/dictionary/">Meet the offline dictionary ↗</a>
      </div>
      <figure>
        <div class="mount shot dictionary-shot">
          <img class="shot-light" src="/salon/dictionary-light.webp" width="1280" height="856" alt="The offline dictionary in Leaflogue desktop defining deliberately, with English meanings, parts of speech, and synonyms." loading="lazy">
          <img class="shot-dark" src="/salon/dictionary-dark.webp" width="1280" height="856" alt="The same offline dictionary in Leaflogue desktop’s dark theme." loading="lazy">
        </div>
        <figcaption class="cartel"><span class="no">No. 17</span><span>A real lookup in Leaflogue desktop · WordNet 3.1</span></figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ——— Room IV: the blue room. A room of your own. ——— -->
<section class="room room-blue sect" aria-labelledby="own-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="kicker"><span>IV</span> A room of your own</p>
    <h2 id="own-title">A private collection, not a public gallery.</h2>
    <p class="intro">What you underline says a lot about you. Leaflogue keeps it on your computer, and anything more is your choice.</p>
    <div class="pillars">
      <article class="pillar"><span class="n">i.</span><h3>It lives on your computer</h3><p>Your books, passages and notes are saved in one SQLite file in your data folder. There's no account to create, and it works offline.</p></article>
      <article class="pillar"><span class="n">ii.</span><h3>You run the sync</h3><p>Sync is optional. It goes to a Convex or PostgreSQL database that you set up, and it catches up whenever Leaflogue opens.</p></article>
      <article class="pillar"><span class="n">iii.</span><h3>AI only when you ask</h3><p>AI is off until you turn it on. The first time, Leaflogue shows you exactly what would leave your computer before anything is sent.</p></article>
    </div>
    <div class="faq">
      <details><summary>Can I look up words without the internet?</summary><p>Yes. Leaflogue desktop includes an English dictionary. Select a word in a passage and choose Define, or use the dictionary button in the header. The data is bundled with the app; no text is sent to a server. <a href="/library/dictionary/">Read the dictionary guide.</a></p></details>
      <details><summary>Does it change my e-reader?</summary><p>No. Leaflogue only reads your highlights and never writes anything back to the device.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What if I import the same file twice?</summary><p>Passages are matched to the ones you already have, so nothing is duplicated. If a passage changed in both places, you choose which version to keep.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Which computers does it run on?</summary><p>The beta targets Linux x64 and ARM64, Apple Silicon and Intel Macs, and Windows x64. Check the release notes for each build’s validation and signing status.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Do I need an account?</summary><p>No. Sync and AI are optional, and both use services you choose and set up yourself.</p></details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ——— Room V: back to red. The way out. ——— -->
<section class="room room-red close" id="download" aria-labelledby="dl-title">
  <div class="wrap close-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="kicker" style="color:inherit;opacity:.75"><span>V</span> The doors are open</p>
      <h2 id="dl-title">Start hanging your own wall.</h2>
      <p class="intro">Plug in your reader, choose Import, and see a year of reading on one page. It's free, open source and yours to keep.</p>
      <div class="ctas">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="https://github.com/Ka1Thakkar/leaflogue/releases" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Get the desktop beta <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
        <a class="btn btn-quiet" href="https://github.com/Ka1Thakkar/leaflogue" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View the source</a>
      </div>
      <p class="fine">Beta targets: Linux · macOS · Windows</p>
    </div>
    <figure>
      <div class="mount"><img src="/salon/panini-vista.webp" width="760" height="1090" alt="A detail of Panini's painted gallery: marble columns, a seated statue and paintings hung up to the ceiling." loading="lazy"></div>
      <figcaption>Detail, <i>Modern Rome</i>, Giovanni Paolo Panini, 1757</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>
